A||||3||Homicidal||Can anybody explain how the battery and alternator work together?||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||05-06-2000||09:14 PM||||I am curious how these two work together!||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||05-06-2000||09:21 PM||||The regulator senses battery voltage and tells the alternator how much to put out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||05-06-2000||09:28 PM||||And an alternator will not charge a dead battery. It will "surface" charge, but not fully.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||05-07-2000||02:42 AM||||The rotating coils inside the alternator is called the rotor.  When power is fed to the rotor a magnetic field is created that spins with the rotor.<P>The stationary coils on the outside of the alternator is called the stator.  When energy from the engine is used to force the magnetic field through the stator electric power flows in the coils.<P>The larger the input voltage to the rotor the higher the output voltage from the stator and the more engine power required to push the field through.<P>An electric valve opens and closes in response to battery voltage, the less battery voltage available the more voltage the valve allows to the rotor.  The circle here is straightforward.<P>When an electric load (headlights,heater,stereo,etc.) drops the voltage the alternator produces more voltage in return until the voltage averages back to about 14 volts.<P>The real world circuits used to achieve the degree of control necessary are more involved than this but the net result is the same.<P>I hope you're glad you asked, I try not to be obscure but according to my kids more often than not I just confuse things.||||reg||12
